{"qid":"Q833","names":["Malaysia"],"title":"Malaysia","endonym":{"native":"Malaysia","lang":"ms"},"capsule":"Malaysia was formed on 16 September 1963 through the federation of Malaya, Singapore, Sabah, and Sarawak, with Singapore expelled in 1965 leaving a federation of eleven peninsular states plus Sabah and Sarawak on Borneo. The country achieved rapid industrialization under Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad (1981–2003), transforming from an agricultural economy into a middle-income industrial state with electronics and palm oil as leading exports. Kuala Lumpur serves as capital, while the federal administrative center was relocated to Putrajaya in 1999; Malaysia's population of approximately 33 million is multiethnic, with Malay-Muslim plurality.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Malaysia's international boundaries are defined by treaty and well documented, though the maritime boundary with the Philippines in Sabah waters and the Pedra Branca area near Singapore involve ongoing disputes.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Malaysia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaysia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: History of Malaysia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Malaysia"}],"britannica":"place/Malaysia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Malaysia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q296317","name":"Tunku Abdul Rahman","start":1957,"end":1970,"image":"Tunku abd rahman.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tunku%20abd%20rahman.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":null,"attributionRequired":false}},{"qid":"Q4200614","name":"Syed Putra of Perlis","start"